title: Running a sync
tags: Sync
kind: reference
summary: mf sync run carries the plan out — links, then each link's metadata before its files — with every write fenced by the version it expects, and records each link it fully synced.

!! Order

# The `create-link` ops: bare records are created at the uuid the plan gave them, then the links. Every reference written afterwards may lead to one.
# The links, parents first (by the depth of their path), one at a time: its `sync`, then its `copy`, `move`, `chmod`; then the link is committed ([[Why a run commits each link on its own]]).
# The `delete` and `drop-link` ops.

''Metadata gates the disk'': a link's files are touched only once its `sync` went
through. A record a sync creates exists at its path before its file does, so the
watcher's event for the file finds it rather than creating a second one ([[Event semantics]]).

!! Writing a copy

A file or a symlink is never written at its destination: the run writes it beside,
under the destination's name followed by `.metafolder-unfinished-sync`, and only once
it is whole does the old entry go to the trash and the copy take its name, by a
rename. A run stopped half-way thus leaves the destination as it was.

Those two steps are ''held'': the run asks the destination's watcher to keep the
path's events buffered (`POST …/watch/hold`) before the trash, and releases them
after the rename, whatever happened (`POST …/watch/release`, [[Watcher endpoints]]).
Without it, a flush falling between the two — likely under load, the trash writing
its index — applied the old file's departure alone: the record was orphaned, the
run's refresh refused, the link left with both sides changed, a conflict, and the
copy arrived as a new record. Held, the departure is applied once the new file is
there, and finds it: the record keeps its identity. Nothing is dropped — a real change
made meanwhile is applied after the release. A hold does not expire: a run that died
inside one leaves the path in `mf watch status`, and `mf watch release` lets it go; a
reload of the repository releases everything. A truncated file
there would read, at the next plan, as a change of that side too, and become a conflict
on `mfr_content` instead of a copy to redo ([[Change detection in sync]]).

A name ending in `.metafolder-unfinished-sync` is never tracked — by the watcher, by
reconcile, nor by the scan of a directory that arrived whole —: what a stopped run left
under it is the run's own, and the next copy to that destination writes over it. A
directory needs no such step: making it is one operation.

!! Versions

The run knows, for each record it touches, the version it expects: the plan's
baseline, then the version its own last write left. Every field write carries it
(`?expected_version=`, [[Conditional writes]]); every file operation first checks
that the record is still at it, and after changing the file makes the record agree
with it ([[Suppressing sync's echoes]]). A record found elsewhere — the user, or
the watcher, wrote meanwhile — makes that operation skip: it stays in the plan, the
link's remaining file operations do not run, and the link is not recorded. The
change is not lost: the next plan sees it.

!! Recording a link

A link whose `sync` went through, and whose two records now agree on everything
sync carries, is ''committed'': the sync database records the two versions the run
expects and a snapshot of what the two sides hold ([[The sync database]]). The run
never re-reads a version to record it — a change that came after its last write is
then still a change at the next sync, instead of being declared in sync without
having reached the other side.

A link is left uncommitted, for the next plan, when one of its operations was
skipped, when an external tool's content diverges ([[What sync copies]]), when a
conflict was skipped, or when a reference had no counterpart. The next plan then
meets the same state and decides the same way.

!! What is left

Each operation that succeeds is deleted from the plan, as are the conflicts of a
committed link; the plan keeps what did not happen. The run prints how many
operations were done and skipped (each skip with its reason on the way), the
subtrees where an external tool's files diverge — a count per top-level directory,
never one line per file —, and suggests `mf reconcile` on both repositories for any
drift the watcher has not reported yet.

A run stopped in the middle loses nothing it did: what was written stays, and its
links are simply not committed. Running the same plan again tolerates the links
and bare records already made; an operation whose record its first attempt had
already changed is skipped. The next `mf sync plan` sees each uncommitted link
afresh, and plans only what is still to do.
